window.docContents[65].resolve({"/Design-restrictions/Behaviour/#Core-C______--Design-restrictions--Behaviour":{"contents":"C++17 defines undefined behaviour as behaviour for which the standard imposes no requirements (N4659 §3.27). An expression whose result is not mathematically defined or not representable in its type has undefined behaviour (N4659 §8 ¶4), and so has a zero divisor (N4659 §8.6 ¶4). So does a delete through a pointer to a base without a virtual destructor (N4659 §8.3.5 ¶3). The end of the storage duration of an object makes every pointer to it invalid. Indirection through an invalid pointer and its deallocation by a second delete have undefined behaviour (N4659 §6.7 ¶4).Core C++ admits none of these cases. Each one fails a premise of every rule that could reach it, so the subject has no derivation. The evaluator reports error with the exit code 134, .The restriction gives every well typed program one of three outcomes, a value, error, or no termination. The semantics fixes the outcome, and no compiler chooses it. The examples of the repository show the difference, through bin/compare.| Example | g++ | Core C++ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| overflow.cpp | 0 | 134 |\n| division_by_zero.cpp | 0 | 134 |\n| out_of_bounds.cpp | 0 | 134 |\n| null_dereference.cpp | 139 | 134 |\n| double_delete.cpp | 133 | 134 |\n| delete_nonvirtual.cpp | 1 | 134 |A non terminating program has no derivation either. This is a limitation of the inductive big step style, not a restriction of the subset (Kahn, RR-0601, §4.4, p. 11).\n\nIn C++17 the evaluations of the operands of most operators are unsequenced (N4659 §4.6 ¶17). The initialisations of the parameters of a call are indeterminately sequenced with respect to each other (N4659 §8.2.2 ¶5). A side effect on a memory location that is unsequenced relative to another side effect or a value computation on that location has undefined behaviour (N4659 §4.6 ¶17).Core C++ evaluates left to right wherever C++17 leaves the order open, . It keeps the orders C++17 fixes, such as the right operand before the left one in an assignment (N4659 §8.18 ¶1).The restriction makes the order of the premises of each rule part of the rule. The store of one premise is the input of the next, so a construct has one applicable rule and one result, .\n\n","context":"Core C++\u0009Design restrictions","header":"9.1. Behaviour","id":"/Design-restrictions/Behaviour/#Core-C______--Design-restrictions--Behaviour"}});